        "timestamp": 1777301117,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. UK, EU 'push back strongly' on US foreign policy: Will Charles' visit 'soften' transatlantic divide? Americas Issued on: 27/04/2026 - 16:45 Modified: 27/04/2026 - 16:48 Annette Young is pleased to welcome Laurel Rapp, Director of the US and North America Programme at Chatham House. According to Ms. Rapp, the visit of King Charles to the US, planned prior to the outbreak of the US war on Iran, now unfolds with far higher stakes in the wake of an all-out diplomatic crisis. What was once regarded as a “special relationship”, the US and the UK are being increasingly tested, and shaped, by combative rhetoric amid an unprecedented divide on key strategic issues: Trade, NATO, Greenland, Iran, Ukraine, defence budget, and a host of other points of contention. Trump turns assassination attempts into 'political rocket fuel' 26/04/2026 Americas Bus blast in Colombia kills 13 amid rising violence 26/04/2026 Americas What we know about the suspect in White House press dinner shooting 26/04/2026 Americas US-Iran talks in doubt after Trump scraps Pakistan visit 26/04/2026 Americas A look at previous attempts on President Trump's life 26/04/2026 Americas LA teacher suspect for White House press dinner shooting 26/04/2026 Americas Choas at White House Correspondents' Dinner as shots ring out 26/04/2026 Americas Trump attends White House Correspondents' Dinner for first time as president 25/04/2026 Americas Peru police raid ex-election chief’s home amid widening probe 25/04/2026 Americas"
